<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

*You are here: the machinery is done (15-22); the last three lessons are what people see, and what gets retired.*

In lessons 10 to 12 you hand-built a LiveView: streams, forms, PubSub, actor from the socket. Every piece of it was derivable from what the resource already knew: attributes, types, actions, policies, notifications. `ash_a2ui` derives it.

It emits [A2UI](https://github.com/a2ui-project/a2ui) protocol messages (v0.9.1 default, v1.0 opt-in per surface) that any A2UI renderer turns into UI: a browser via the shipped LiveView hook, an agent canvas, a chat surface. Client interactions come back as `action` envelopes routed into **your Ash actions**, with the actor and policies applied. That is lessons 10 to 12, as data.

<div class="rounded-xl p-4 my-4" style="background-color: #fdf6ec; border-left: 4px solid #c2410c; color: #7c2d12;">
  <p><i class="ri-fire-fill"></i> <strong>Soot was here.</strong> The desk has drawn itself. I watched three lessons of hand-built LiveView become a declaration, and the declaration is shorter.</p>
</div>

## Declare a surface

Keep UI out of the shared domain resource with a standalone module:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise_web/a2ui/refund_ui.ex
defmodule AshEnterpriseWeb.A2ui.RefundUI do
  use AshA2ui.Standalone

  a2ui do
    for_resource AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund
    surface_id "refunds"

    component :table do
      fields [:ticket_ref, :amount, :status, :created_on]
      read_action :read
      row_actions [:approve, :reject]
    end

    component :form do
      fields [:ticket_ref, :amount, :reason]
      create_action :propose
    end

    field :created_on do
      label "Proposed"
      format :date
    end
  end
end
```

Reference a field or action that does not exist and it fails at compile time. Strictness matters more when you emit a wire protocol.

## See the messages

Build the surface for your actor and look at the stream before rendering anything:

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  AshA2ui.Info.build_surface(AshEnterpriseWeb.A2ui.RefundUI, actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
  ```

  `actor` and `tenant` are lesson 15's fabric (re-run that cell if this is a fresh Livebook session). Build it again as a user without access to `:approve` — lesson 20's checker with its grants removed, say — and the row action is absent. The UI did not decide that; the policy did. Hiding is UX; the policy still refuses the action if a client sends it anyway.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** The surface came back as protocol messages, no HTML written — and an actor without `:approve` would see fewer row actions, not an error.

## Render it

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise_web/a2ui/refund_a2ui_live.ex
defmodule AshEnterpriseWeb.RefundA2uiLive do
  use AshA2ui.LiveRenderer,
    ui: AshEnterpriseWeb.A2ui.RefundUI,
    actor_fn: & &1.assigns.current_user
end
```

Route it — save it at `lib/ash_enterprise_web/a2ui/refund_a2ui_live.ex`, add the route inside the authenticated `live_session`, restart the node (router and DSL edits alike), and open it. Approve a refund from the notebook: the row updates live, because the renderer refreshes from `Ash.Notifier.PubSub` (lesson 12). Add `notifiers: [Ash.Notifier.PubSub]` and a `pub_sub` block to Refund if you have not — same rule, restart after saving.

## Querying is an allowlist

Clients may not send arbitrary sort or filter params. Declare a named query; the client references it by name, and anything else is rejected before Ash is called.

Add a `query :default` with `search_fields [:ticket_ref, :reason]`.

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  query :default do
    search_fields [:ticket_ref, :reason]
  end
  ```

  The full DSL (sorts, filters, pagination, layout, relationships) is in `ash_a2ui`'s `usage-rules/` sub-rules; sync them with `mix usage_rules.sync` and let your agent read `ash_a2ui:queries` on demand.

  </div>
</details>

## Where A2UI stops

`ash_admin` remains the operator back office; A2UI is the experience layer (ADR 0005). Things that are not CRUD-shaped keep their own pages: a BPMN diagram is bpmn-js, a task list is an indexed candidate query. The host declares which projections (`:diagram`, `:history`, ...) an object carries, from a vocabulary the library owns (ADR 0037).

